  "review": {
    "review": "The complete recording confirms death: decision 3474, log 10249, changes HP from 2 to 0 and the terminal public text names a necromancer's skeleton. This is not inferred from nearby enemies; the compact packet omitted that text. Earlier attrition began with the guard pull during loot travel (D3324, logs 9734–9735); committed DM-100 flight then lost 19 HP over D3332–3333. A later, specific planning defect is that necromancer navigation treats a two-square gap from an adrenaline-buffed summon as contact-free. At D3442 (logs 10141–10142), the wounded hero investigates a remembered caster, moving from 108 to 149 while the empowered summon is at 107; HP falls from 23 to 20. The helper uses adjacency checks despite the shared risk evaluator already recognizing fast pursuit. A reduced public-input fixture reproduced this selection, without reconstructing original runtime memory or dispatching actions. No patch is retained: the proposed pursuit veto passed its four new probe cases but failed six existing regression assertions, replacing required spacing with adjacent summon attacks, including at 3 HP. That is not a supported safe replacement. All changes were reverted; the restored full suite passed 2,443 tests with four skips. No planner incidents were recorded. The exact enemy scheduling and a survival-preserving alternative remain unestablished.",
    "learnings": "Account for publicly visible adrenaline when comparing necromancer approaches and withdrawals, but do not turn every threatened gap into a stationary summon attack. Preserve low-health death-blast spacing, bounded combat, hazard admission and mandatory pickup receipts. A locally admitted shot is not evidence of survival."
  },
